        <briefdescription>
<para>Opens a file as an <ref refid="class_input" kindref="compound">Input</ref>. </para>
        </briefdescription>
        <detaileddescription>
<para>The size comes from std::filesystem::file_size. That call fails for a named pipe, a socket, a device, and a folder on macOS, so none of those is opened. The null device is the one exception: it gives an empty <ref refid="class_input" kindref="compound">Input</ref>. A file larger than maxReadSize is mapped. When the map fails, the file is read into an owned buffer. A smaller file is read into an owned buffer, and a read that returns fewer bytes than the size gives nullptr. Passing allBytes always reads into an owned buffer, so a later write to the file cannot change the bytes. On macOS, a mapped file that another process makes shorter gives no fault: a missing byte reads as NUL.</para>
<para><parameterlist kind="param"><parameteritem>
<parameternamelist>
<parametername>file</parametername>
</parameternamelist>
<parameterdescription>
<para>The file to open. </para>
</parameterdescription>
</parameteritem>
<parameteritem>
<parameternamelist>
<parametername>maxReadSize</parametername>
</parameternamelist>
<parameterdescription>
<para>The largest file size that is read into a buffer.</para>
</parameterdescription>
</parameteritem>
</parameterlist>
<simplesect kind="return"><para>The <ref refid="class_input" kindref="compound">Input</ref>, or nullptr when the file cannot be opened or read. </para>
